# The first dates of the things in the street are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-homes-then-and-now v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=5 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A street then and now"
  goal "Tell a street long ago from a street today by what is in it, and find the thing that could not have been there."

  look {
    scene 32x16 {
      thenandnow street year=1850 sign=1 things=["horse and cart", "hot-air balloon"] at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      This is a street in 1850. The houses look much like houses now, and some old houses are still
      lived in today. What has changed is what goes along the street: in 1850 people rode horses or
      walked, and a balloon was a wonder in the sky. There were no cars, no aeroplanes and no
      phones yet. Each of those was first made at a time we know.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.street-long-ago k=0
    }
    show history.street-long-ago k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
    show history.street-could-not k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show history.street-newest k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.street-could-not k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.street-long-ago k=2 easy-k=0 level=medium hard-k=3
    show history.street-could-not k=2 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.street-newest k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
  }
  story {
    show history.street-how-many-new k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show history.street-how-many-new k=1 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.houses-not-the-clue k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.street-which-year k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "To tell when a picture was made, look for the newest thing in it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a picture can be dated by what is in it: every thing was first made at some
    time, so the newest thing in a street says the street cannot be older than that. The way in
    tells long ago from today, the core finds the thing that could not have been there in the year
    on the sign and the newest thing in today's street, and the story counts everything too new.
    The wrong idea to watch for is dating a picture by its houses: old houses stand in streets
    today, so they say little. If the child is stuck, ask of each thing "did Great-grandma have
    one when she was little?" Off the page, walk down your own street and find the oldest and the
    newest thing in it. England's key stage 1 history asks for "changes within living memory" and
    "events beyond living memory", and this street crosses from one to the other.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example shows a street long ago, the thing that could not be there is far newer than the year on the sign, and the newest thing is a phone or an aerial among old things."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the years on the sign are 1920, 1950 and 1870, so the too-new thing is only a few decades out, the newest thing is chosen among three newer ones, a second counting question is added, and the three-star question has three late years to choose between."
  }
}
